# Deep visual replication before applying UI changes

When asked to "apply"/"replicate" a reference site's UI/design system into a codebase, a shallow
token pass (background color, radius, font) is not enough — do a full feature inventory before
touching CSS: side nav and any "on this page" TOC, tab controls, per-block action buttons, and
full-surface theme sync across every affected component, not just the shell.

**Why:** a tokens-only pass was once called out as "a very shallow replica" — it missed a TOC,
missed action-button chrome, and left child components (e.g. tables) unsynced with a new dark
theme. A narrow pass that's correct for research/analysis is not automatically correct for the
*apply* step — research can stay narrow, implementation must cover every visible surface changed.

**How to apply:**
1. Enumerate every distinct chrome element visible in reference screenshots — don't stop at
   colors/radius/font.
2. Search the target app for every component/file whose styling is affected by the change (not
   just the shell file) before calling a theme/style change "applied."
3. If a reference feature has no natural equivalent in the target, say so explicitly and ask
   rather than silently omitting it.
